/**
  @page examples-index Examples and tutorials
  @brief Guided numerical problems and application source examples.

  @tableofcontents

  The tutorials explain a problem, its discretization, the code that realizes
  it, and the interpretation of the result. Source examples extend that
  workflow to other models and backends.

  @section examples-index-start Start here

  1. @ref guides-getting-started-first-steps "Build a first program" to create
     a mesh and a finite element space.
  2. @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Solve the Poisson equation" to connect a weak
     form to assembly, a solver, and visualization.
  3. @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "Continue with linear elasticity" for a
     vector-valued field and boundary traction.

  The @ref guides-index "General concepts" guides supply the mathematical
  background. The @ref gallery "Gallery" collects visual results.

  @section examples-index-variational Before choosing a model

  A variational problem combines a trial space, a test space, forms, and
  boundary conditions. The @ref guides-variational "variational formulations
  guide" explains this construction; the @ref guides-solvers "solver guide"
  explains how the resulting matrix properties determine solver choice.

  @section examples-index-pdes Solving PDEs

  Begin with Poisson, then select a tutorial according to the field type,
  boundary conditions, or geometry needed for the problem.

  - @subpage examples-pdes-index — Scalar diffusion, elasticity, waves,
    incompressible flow, periodic conditions, surface equations and DG.

  @section examples-index-io Input/output

  Rodin supports several I/O formats for meshes, grid functions, and simulation
  data. The supported formats are @ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "MFEM", MEDIT,
  and HDF5. The @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "XDMF" format is recommended for
  exporting results for visualization in ParaView:

  - @subpage examples-io-index

  @section examples-index-geometry Mesh geometry

  The @ref Rodin::Geometry "Geometry" module provides mesh creation,
  topological connectivity, and geometric operations:

  - @subpage examples-geometry-index

  @section examples-index-mmg Working with MMG

  The @ref Rodin::MMG "MMG" module has a wide variety of uses which
  ranges from optimization of the mesh, to implicit domain meshing. It provides
  many C++ wrappers to execute the functionality of the [Mmg
  Platform](https://www.mmgtools.org/):

  - @subpage examples-mmg-index

  @section examples-index-density_optimization Density optimization examples

  - @subpage examples-density_optimization-index

  @section examples-index-shapeoptimization Shape optimization examples

  - @subpage examples-shapeoptimization-index

  @section examples-index-solid Solid mechanics source examples

  The @ref Rodin::Solid "Solid" module provides hyperelastic constitutive
  laws and nonlinear integrators for large-deformation solid mechanics. The
  @ref Rodin::Variational::LinearElasticityIntegral "LinearElasticityIntegral" covers the small-deformation
  (linearized) case, while the Solid module handles finite-strain problems with
  constitutive laws such as NeoHookean, Saint-Venant-Kirchhoff, and
  Mooney-Rivlin:

  | Problem | Constitutive Law | Example |
  |---------|-----------------|---------|
  | Linear cantilever beam | @f$ \sigma = \lambda(\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon)I + 2\mu\varepsilon @f$ | @ref examples-pdes-elasticity |
  | Hyperelastic cantilever (NeoHookean, transient) | @f$ W = \frac{\mu}{2}(I_1-d) - \mu\ln J + \frac{\lambda}{2}(\ln J)^2 @f$ | [CantileverBeam.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/Solid/CantileverBeam.cpp) |

  @section examples-index-eikonal Distance and Eikonal

  The @ref Rodin::Eikonal "Eikonal" module solves @f$ F(x)|\nabla u| = 1 @f$ for positive propagation speed @f$F@f$
  via the Fast Marching Method, and the @ref Rodin::Distance "Distance" module
  wraps several distance-function computation strategies:

  | Method | Description | Example |
  |--------|-------------|---------|
  | FMM | Arrival times, exact point-source comparison and mesh refinement | @ref examples-eikonal-fmm |
  | Spalding–Tucker | Signed auxiliary Poisson field and normalization error | @ref examples-distance-normalization |

  @section examples-index-petsc PETSc source examples

  The @ref Rodin::PETSc "PETSc" module enables distributed solvers and
  multi-field block systems. Examples include:

  | Problem | Description | Example |
  |---------|-------------|---------|
  | Sequential Poisson | PETSc CG solver on local mesh | [Seq_Poisson.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_Poisson.cpp) |
  | MPI Poisson | Distributed mesh + PETSc KSP | [MPI_Poisson.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/MPI_Poisson.cpp) |
  | Sequential Stokes | Multi-field (velocity-pressure-Lagrange) | [Seq_Stokes.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_Stokes.cpp) |
  | Oseen flow | Transient Picard/Oseen linearization | [Seq_OseenFlow.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_OseenFlow.cpp) |
  | Oseen lid-driven cavity | Transient cavity with Picard/Oseen linearization | [Seq_OseenLidDrivenCavity.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_OseenLidDrivenCavity.cpp) |
  | Navier-Stokes | Transient nonlinear lid-driven cavity with PETSc SNES | [Seq_NavierStokes.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_NavierStokes.cpp) |

  @see @ref guides-petsc "PETSc guide", @ref guides-mpi "MPI guide"

  @section examples-index-dg Discontinuous Galerkin

  The @ref examples-pdes-dg-poisson "SIPG diffusion tutorial" assembles
  independent cell-local affine unknowns, interior consistency and penalty
  terms, and weak Dirichlet data. It includes an affine patch test and a
  four-level refinement study.

  The @ref examples-interface-traces "trace tutorial" first evaluates the
  two one-sided values, jump and average on a two-cell mesh.

  @section examples-index-integral Integral equation source examples

  The `Potential` operator enables integral
  equation formulations (boundary element methods). The kernel
  @f$ K(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) @f$ is convolved with the solution on the mesh:

  | Problem | Kernel | Example |
  |---------|--------|---------|
  | Newtonian potential | @f$ \frac{1}{4\pi\lVert\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\rVert} @f$ | [NewtonianPotential.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/IntegralEquations/NewtonianPotential.cpp) |
  | Elastic distribution | Navier 3×3 kernel | [ElasticDistribution.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/IntegralEquations/ElasticDistribution.cpp) |

  @section examples-index-surfevol Surface evolution source examples

  The surface evolution examples demonstrate conormal advection — moving
  domain boundaries along their outward normal direction using level-set
  methods with MMG implicit-domain meshing:

  | Problem | Description | Example |
  |---------|-------------|---------|
  | Conormal advection | Move boundary along conormal | [Main.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/SurfaceEvolution/ConormalAdvection/Main.cpp) |
  | Convergence study | Parametric sweep over resolution and CFL | [Test.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/SurfaceEvolution/ConormalAdvection/Test.cpp) |
  | Plane evolution | Planar surface motion | [Plane.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/SurfaceEvolution/ConormalAdvection/Plane.cpp) |

  @section examples-index-boundaryopt Boundary optimization source examples

  Advanced shape and topology optimization examples that combine complex-valued
  PDE solvers, adjoint methods, Hilbert space regularization, and MMG
  remeshing:

  | Problem | Description | Example |
  |---------|-------------|---------|
  | Acoustic cloaking | Complex Helmholtz + adjoint-based shape gradient + conormal advection | [AcousticCloaking.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/BoundaryOptimization/AcousticCloaking.cpp) |

  */
